WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.690
Let’s practice how to share changes with your care team.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:11.050
Tell your care team about symptoms that are new, keep coming back, or are getting worse.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:23.020
A body map can help you name the area: skin, nails, hands, wrists, shoulders, elbows, back, hips, knees, ankles, or feet.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:30.520
Also mention tiredness, and changes in walking, sleep, work, or self-care.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:37.300
A useful message says where the problem is, when it began, and what has changed.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:41.510
For example: new knee pain makes stairs harder.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:47.120
A body map cannot tell you how urgent a symptom is.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:54.550
Medicine concerns, and repeated flares, are also reasons to contact your prescribing team.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:01.160
Ask your clinic for its phone number, office hours, and how fast they reply.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:05.490
Plan ahead, and ask how to get help when the clinic is closed.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:13.540
For urgent symptoms, use urgent medical services. Don’t wait for a routine app message.

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:17.610
Great job! Let’s review.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:23.190
A useful message says where the problem is, when it began, and what has changed.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:27.600
A body map cannot tell you how urgent a symptom is.
